  "spec_section": "protocol.md §9.22 + §5.2 + §6.4 + §10.3.1",
  "description": "Pins protocol::vault_label_blob::{encode_vault_label_blob_raw, decode_vault_label_blob} byte mapping for the MYL1 sealed label store (vault.label, §9.22, myne-md/myne#411). One file per vault at the vault root, carrying that vault's human-readable name and nothing else, so the picker can show it BEFORE any unlock (the authoritative label lives in vault.prefs, which is sealed under a root_key-derived KEK and therefore unreadable at that moment). Device-local: never synced, never backed up. Sealed under a PER-VAULT 32-byte wrap_key held in the OS keychain (service md.myne.vaultlabel), NOT a DeriveSubkey(root_key, ...) subkey, so there is NO HKDF SubkeyRole and the vectors supply wrap_key directly. Per-vault rather than device-global because vault deletion carries a per-VaultId cryptographic-erase guarantee: one key per vault is what lets deleting a vault destroy exactly one name. Unlike its device-local siblings MYK1 (§9.18) and MYF1 (§9.20) this family is NOT vault-agnostic: aad_vault_label_blob binds the 16-byte VaultId the READER EXPECTED, not merely the one the blob carries, which closes structurally the copied-to-another-name gap those two leave to a reader-side check. ciphertext_len is variable (plaintext_len + 32) with a 4 KiB plaintext cap (§9.22.4), enforced on both sides: an oversize ciphertext_len is rejected before allocation, and an over-cap label is refused at write time rather than truncated. §10.3.1 strict-trailing is ENFORCED (blob length must equal header + ciphertext_len exactly), unlike the older device-local families. 18 cases: 1 aad_construction + 3 encrypt (ascii, multibyte, at the 4096-byte cap) + 1 encode_fail (one byte over the cap) + 2 decrypt_ok (ascii, multibyte) + 3 decrypt_aead_fail (tag-tamper, relabelled header vault_id, wrong wrap_key, all non-distinguishing per §6.4) + 8 parse_fail (one per distinguishable pre-decryption structural reject). Every parse_fail fixture carries the REAL wrap_key, so a port that skips a structural check decodes Ok and the runner catches it rather than the bug hiding behind a coincidental AEAD failure.",
